    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d396">

                <pb facs="0552" n="536" sameAs="frus:frus1948v09/pg536"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.00/12–1148: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Costa Rica</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">San José</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-12-11T17:00:00-06:00">December 11,
                            1948—5 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>396. President sent for me 3 p. m. With Ulate and Foreign Minister present asked
                    if US would permit Costa Rica purchase arms in event invasion not speedily
                        repulsed.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d396-f1"/> I replied my understanding US had
                    not refused his government export license for arms procurable private sources,
                    although for some time US Government itself unable sell. On his repeated
                    request, seconded by Ulate, I promised inquire possibility obtain 2000 rifles,
                    100 light machine guns using same ammunition, <pb facs="0553" n="537"
                        xml:id="pg537"/> 100 medium mchten [<hi rendition="#italic">machine?</hi>]
                    AA guns and armament for <hi rendition="#italic">P–38</hi>. Please wire
                        reply.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d396-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Replying my direct question said Caribbean Legion offered support as unit with
                    600 men. This proposal rejected but stated individual volunteers acceptable, and
                    he is demanding delivery all Legion arms which would be taken forcibly if not
                    given up by tonight. Emphasized while Legion has arms in Costa Rica for that
                    number, actual membership far less.</p>
                <p>Said no reports any support within Costa Rica of invasion, on contrary offers
                    pouring in aid government. Endeavoring detain Vanguardia leaders but all except
                    one in hiding.</p>
                <p>Has instructed Marten<anchor n="3" xml:id="d396-f3"/> invoke Rio treaty.<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d396-f4"/> While his information sketchy, understands 800 men
                    in attack La Cruz. Says only about 200 can possibly be Ticas.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d396-f1" n="1" xml:id="d396-f1-content">
                <p>The northwest frontier of Costa Rica was reported to have been invaded on
                    December 10, allegedly by armed forces from Nicaragua.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d396-f2" n="2" xml:id="d396-f2-content">
                <p>Acting Secretary Lovett replied in telegram 267, December 12, not printed, as
                    follows: “As Costa Rican Govt already informed US Govt has no surplus arms
                    available for sale but no objection granting export licenses for approved
                    purchases Costa Rican Govt. Reurtel 396, Dec. 11.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d396-f3" n="3" xml:id="d396-f3-content">
                <p>Alberto Marten, Minister of Economy and Finance, who was in Washington on
                    government business at that time.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d396-f4" n="4" xml:id="d396-f4-content">
                <p>The Inter-American treaty of reciprocal assistance, signed at Rio de Janeiro,
                    September 2, 1947, effective December 3, 1948 (Department of State Treaties and
                    other International Acts Series 1838, or 62 Stat. (2) 1681).</p>
                <p>For documentation on this subject, see <ref target="frus1947v08#pg_1">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1947, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">viii</hi>, pp. 1</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
